  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 350 AM CDT Wed Sep 30 2026\n\nA strong upper low in the subtropical stream over the southwest\nCONUS will track east across the Southern Plains during the next \ncouple of days, slowly filling as it does so. Ahead of the upper \nlow, an upper level ridge will remain strong in our region, and \nthis will bring another day of dry boundary layer conditions. Strong \nmixing with critically low relative humidity will support a limited \nthreat for wildfires over northeast portions of the area. Given how \ndry it has been recently, we will resume the formal fire danger \nmessaging for today. \n\nA change to a wetter weather pattern is still anticipated for \nlate this week into the weekend as the previously mentioned trough \nhelps to induce increased southerly flow and moisture transport, \nwith deep level moisture eventually increasing to anomalous levels \nover the forecast area (e.g., precipitable water 2 to 3 SD positive \nanomalies). Given the prolonged presence of weak synoptic scale \nascent related to the subtle but persistent upper jet influence and \nmodest low level moisture convergence, the forecast reflects a long \nduration of high rain chances in the Friday through Sunday time \nframe. \n\nWhile prospects for a wet weekend remain high, the potential impacts\nrelated to this upcoming wet weather pattern are quite uncertain,\nespecially when considering that model guidance has continued to \nshift weaker with the upper trough presence and stronger with the \nsoutheast CONUS subtropical high center. Because of this, heavier \nQPF has shifted some closer to the coast and brings into question \nthe potential for heavier rainfall. Regardless of the heavy rain\npotential, given how dry it has been recently, the rain that does\nfall would be highly beneficial in most cases. \n\nLooking ahead, drier and somewhat cooler air should surge into \nthe forecast area as northwest flow aloft becomes the primary \ndriver for our weather. /EC/",
